    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d329-a1">

                <pb facs="0445" n="349" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg349"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 117.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Choate to Lord Salisbury.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1899-07-11"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">July 11,
                    1899</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Lord</hi>: It affords me the greatest pleasure to
                    inform your lordship that I have received the instructions of the President to
                    express to your lordship the cordial thanks of the Government of the United
                    States for the very friendly offices performed on its behalf by Her Majesty’s
                    diplomatic and consular representatives in Spain during the recent war between
                    the United States and that country; and the President desires especially to
                    express his obligations to Sir Henry Drummond Wolff, Her Majesty’s ambassador at
                    the Court of Madrid, for his faithful attention to the interests of the United
                    States and of its citizens during the suspension of diplomatic relations.</p>
                <p>At the outset of the war Her Majesty’s representatives accredited to the Spanish
                    Government very generously undertook to look after American interests in Spain,
                    and to transact for the United States such public business as the existence of
                    war rendered possible and necessary.</p>
                <p>The services so voluntarily assumed were uniformly performed in the most friendly
                    and courteous manner, and the President and people of the United States fully
                    appreciate their value, and cherish a grateful recollection of them. They were a
                    happy earnest of the good will prevailing between the two countries.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph H. Choate.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
